/span> <span data-s=\"74\" data-w=\"47\">volumes.</span></p>\n<p><strong><span data-s=\"75\" data-w=\"0\">Common</span> <span data-s=\"75\" data-w=\"1\">mistakes</span></strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span data-s=\"76\" data-w=\"0\">Quoting</span> <span data-s=\"76\" data-w=\"1\">accuracy</span> <span data-s=\"76\" data-w=\"2\">on</span> <span data-s=\"76\" data-w=\"3\">imbalanced</span> <span data-s=\"76\" data-w=\"4\">data.</span></li>\n<li><span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"0\">Optimizing</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"1\">a</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"2\">metric</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"3\">that</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"4\">is</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"5\">not</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"6\">what</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"7\">the</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"8\">business</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"9\">pays</span> <span data-s=\"77\" data-w=\"10\">for.</span></li>\n<li><span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"0\">Tuning</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"1\">the</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"2\">threshold</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"3\">or</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"4\">hyperparameters</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"5\">on</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"6\">the</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"7\">test</span> <span data-s=\"78\" data-w=\"8\">set.</span></li>\n<li><span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"0\">Comparing</span> <span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"1\">two</span> <span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"2\">models</span> <span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"3\">on</span> <span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"4\">different</span> <span data-s=\"79\" data-w=\"5\">splits.</span></li>\n<li><span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"0\">Reporting</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"1\">one</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"2\">number</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"3\">with</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"4\">no</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"5\">sense</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"6\">of</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"7\">its</span> <span data-s=\"80\" data-w=\"8\">spread.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check yourself</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A fraud model has 99% accuracy and the fraud rate is 1%. Is it good? What do you check?</li>\n<li>TP = 20, FP = 30, FN = 10. Compute precision and recall.</li>\n<li>You lower the threshold from 0.5 to 0.3. What happens to precision and recall, and why?</li>\n<li>Why split loan data by date instead of randomly?</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"answers\"><summary>Show answers</summary><p> (1) Not necessarily: always predicting \"no fraud\" scores 99%. Check recall and precision for the fraud class, and compare with the baseline. (2) Precision = 20 / 50 = 0.40; recall = 20 / 30 = 0.67. (3) Recall rises and precision usually falls, because more cases are flagged, including more wrong ones. (4) A random split lets the model train on data from after the cases it is tested on, which flatters it. Production only ever predicts the future.</p></details>\n<p><strong>Go deeper:</strong> the scikit-learn guides on <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">model evaluation</a>, <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cross-validation</a> and <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">probability calibration</a> · the <a href=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.confusion_matrix.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">confusion_matrix</a> reference · Google's <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/rules-of-ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rules of Machine Learning</a>.</p>\n"}